00:06:30.050 --> 00:06:32.870
by different customers
with different kinds

00:06:32.870 --> 00:06:35.870
of use cases and [INAUDIBLE].

00:06:35.870 --> 00:06:38.810
So for today's roadmap
session, what we did was

00:06:38.810 --> 00:06:41.990
we have taken some of the key
highlights in the roadmap,

00:06:41.990 --> 00:06:43.610
and we want to
share them with you.

00:06:43.610 --> 00:06:46.027
And we are going to do this
across the three value pillars

00:06:46.027 --> 00:06:47.960
that we have-- being
open and flexible,

00:06:47.960 --> 00:06:51.200
intelligent, and with
proven dependability.

00:06:53.740 --> 00:06:56.320
TINO TERESHKO: Let's start
with proven dependability.

00:06:56.320 --> 00:06:59.350
HSBC and Sky, of course,
multinational corporations

00:06:59.350 --> 00:07:01.930
that put their
trust into BigQuery

00:07:01.930 --> 00:07:03.680
to drive their business forward.

00:07:03.680 --> 00:07:06.670
So we really need to take care
of our customers in this area.

00:07:06.670 --> 00:07:10.000
And so what we think about it
is we enhance dependability

00:07:10.000 --> 00:07:11.290
in four areas--

00:07:11.290 --> 00:07:14.800
availability and uptime,
administration and control,

00:07:14.800 --> 00:07:16.970
performance, and
just query execution,

00:07:16.970 --> 00:07:21.990
and, finally, trust,
security, and governance.

00:07:21.990 --> 00:07:24.930
Today, we're announcing the
fact that we are enhancing

00:07:24.930 --> 00:07:27.420
our SLA by an additional nine.

00:07:27.420 --> 00:07:31.230
So now there's four nines
availability that we guarantee.

00:07:31.230 --> 00:07:34.110
We're doing this free of
charge to all of our customers.

00:07:34.110 --> 00:07:36.390
There's no special rates
that you have to pay.

00:07:36.390 --> 00:07:39.390
The reason we're able
to do this is because we

00:07:39.390 --> 00:07:40.750
have serverless architecture.

00:07:40.750 --> 00:07:44.310
It's a very advanced
radically unique architecture

00:07:44.310 --> 00:07:47.010
that does guarantee
against downtime

00:07:47.010 --> 00:07:48.820
in specific circumstances.

00:07:48.820 --> 00:07:50.730
So these four nines
of availability

00:07:50.730 --> 00:07:54.450
is really only about four
minutes of downtime per month,

00:07:54.450 --> 00:07:56.550
or less than an hour per day.

00:07:56.550 --> 00:07:59.730
And you know that it is actually
industry leading when it

00:07:59.730 --> 00:08:01.830
comes to cloud data warehouses.

00:08:01.830 --> 00:08:08.330
All other warehouses only offer
three nines of availability.

00:08:08.330 --> 00:08:10.080
And, of course, we
care about performance,

00:08:10.080 --> 00:08:12.690
and our customers care
about performance.

00:08:12.690 --> 00:08:15.270
So we started running this
query in the last few years

00:08:15.270 --> 00:08:17.770
to demonstrate the
power of BigQuery.

00:08:17.770 --> 00:08:20.130
This is a query that is
a simple point look up,

00:08:20.130 --> 00:08:23.770
but it is a query on an
entire petabyte worth of data.

00:08:23.770 --> 00:08:26.670
So in 2016, when we
first ran this query,

00:08:26.670 --> 00:08:28.170
it executed in
about six minutes,

00:08:28.170 --> 00:08:30.540
and it scanned the
whole petabyte.

00:08:30.540 --> 00:08:34.679
In 2018, a couple of years
later, we executed this query

00:08:34.679 --> 00:08:37.080
in just two minutes,
and it actually

00:08:37.080 --> 00:08:39.750
scanned about 400 megabytes.

00:08:39.750 --> 00:08:42.539
Today, if you run this
query in BigQuery,

00:08:42.539 --> 00:08:45.730
you will notice that it takes
about four seconds to run.

00:08:45.730 --> 00:08:48.840
And the best part of
all is that you didn't

00:08:48.840 --> 00:08:50.650
need to do anything special.

00:08:50.650 --> 00:08:52.440
You didn't need to
pay anything extra

00:08:52.440 --> 00:08:54.220
to start getting this
better performance.

00:08:54.220 --> 00:08:56.550
We just upgraded
your service for you

00:08:56.550 --> 00:08:58.650
seamlessly without any
downtime, and you just

00:08:58.650 --> 00:09:01.050
got better performance.

00:09:01.050 --> 00:09:05.400
In fact, just in 2020 alone,
from the beginning of the year,

00:09:05.400 --> 00:09:08.870
we've made major improvements
to all the typical industry

00:09:08.870 --> 00:09:10.110
standard benchmarks.

00:09:10.110 --> 00:09:13.460
This is a TPC-DS benchmark,
and we're looking

00:09:13.460 --> 00:09:16.100
at the geo mean of performance.

00:09:16.100 --> 00:09:19.520
So both for 1T,
10T, and 30T scales,

00:09:19.520 --> 00:09:22.860
we improve performance
anywhere from 20% to 30%.

00:09:22.860 --> 00:09:24.620
And again, you just
get this performance

00:09:24.620 --> 00:09:26.130
as part of using BigQuery.

00:09:26.130 --> 00:09:28.860
You don't have to do
anything, and it just happens.

00:09:32.100 --> 00:09:34.970
So we have a number of
customers in all kinds of areas.

00:09:34.970 --> 00:09:37.820
And, of course, sports
leagues and sports teams

00:09:37.820 --> 00:09:40.190
are taken advantage
of BigQuery--

00:09:40.190 --> 00:09:42.200
major league baseball
being the newest one.

00:09:42.200 --> 00:09:45.650
There's a breakdown, DA204,
that talks about the journey

00:09:45.650 --> 00:09:49.453
that MLB took to kind of
re-platform onto BigQuery.

00:09:49.453 --> 00:09:50.870
Well, the first
thing they noticed

00:09:50.870 --> 00:09:52.703
when they started using
BigQuery is the fact

00:09:52.703 --> 00:09:56.240
that they're able to give this
information, to give analytics

00:09:56.240 --> 00:09:58.880
to all the key stakeholders
within Major League

00:09:58.880 --> 00:10:02.000
Baseball and the teams that
are part of the league.

00:10:02.000 --> 00:10:04.160
And they're able to
do this in real time.

00:10:04.160 --> 00:10:06.050
They don't have these
bottlenecks anymore.

00:10:06.050 --> 00:10:07.425
At the same time,
they're getting

00:10:07.425 --> 00:10:11.430
performance that's significantly
better than the previous--

00:10:11.430 --> 00:10:13.400
the previous platform.

00:10:13.400 --> 00:10:17.810
And finally, it's very, very
easy for them to manage this.

00:10:17.810 --> 00:10:19.550
Because BigQuery is
serverless, there's

00:10:19.550 --> 00:10:22.650
really not a lot of things
for them to worry about,

00:10:22.650 --> 00:10:25.070
and they really like that.

00:10:25.070 --> 00:10:27.160
So we talked about how
BigQuery is serverless it

00:10:27.160 --> 00:10:29.110
doesn't require administration.

00:10:29.110 --> 00:10:32.050
That said, the more
customers we have that

00:10:32.050 --> 00:10:34.460
are multinational,
that are complicated--

00:10:34.460 --> 00:10:36.040
they have lots of
business units--

00:10:36.040 --> 00:10:38.230
the more they're asking
for better control,

00:10:38.230 --> 00:10:40.910
better predictability,
better visibility.

00:10:40.910 --> 00:10:43.210
So we've made the
significant investments here.

00:10:43.210 --> 00:10:45.910
Just in 2020 alone,
we've launched a number

00:10:45.910 --> 00:10:47.480
of improvements to BigQuery.

00:10:47.480 --> 00:10:49.540
The first one
being Reservations.

00:10:49.540 --> 00:10:52.540
Reservations is an enterprise
grade workload management

00:10:52.540 --> 00:10:56.830
platform that allows you to
achieve predictable pricing

00:10:56.830 --> 00:10:59.530
and to make sure that all
your work loads are executing

00:10:59.530 --> 00:11:01.180
under the proper SLAs.

00:11:01.180 --> 00:11:04.870
And it's super efficient because
it's using idle capacity.

00:11:04.870 --> 00:11:07.270
The next enhancement
is flex slots.

00:11:07.270 --> 00:11:09.760
Flex slots allow you to
purchase capacity just

00:11:09.760 --> 00:11:13.360
for a few seconds at a time,
and you can cancel them anytime.

00:11:13.360 --> 00:11:15.040
You essentially get
instant capacity

00:11:15.040 --> 00:11:17.450
to meet your workload demands.

00:11:17.450 --> 00:11:21.510
And for the classic database
administrators out there,

00:11:21.510 --> 00:11:23.870
we've made a number of
enhancements, as well.

00:11:23.870 --> 00:11:25.850
One is information schema.

00:11:25.850 --> 00:11:28.340
These are just tables
that contain metadata

00:11:28.340 --> 00:11:30.320
relevant to your service.

00:11:30.320 --> 00:11:32.240
We've made enhancements
of data types,

00:11:32.240 --> 00:11:35.060
and we've made improvements
to SQL [INAUDIBLE] as well.

00:11:38.053 --> 00:11:39.720
I'm really delighted
about this feature.

00:11:39.720 --> 00:11:41.220
It's coming in the
next few months--

00:11:41.220 --> 00:11:43.890
BigQuery slot autoscaling.

00:11:43.890 --> 00:11:47.600
So this kind of combines
the best of both worlds.

00:11:47.600 --> 00:11:49.640
You're getting the
price predictability

00:11:49.640 --> 00:11:55.810
of slots based pricing, and you
get the low management overhead

00:11:55.810 --> 00:11:58.340
that's associated with
our on demand model.

00:11:58.340 --> 00:12:00.140
BigQuery slots
autoscaling allows

00:12:00.140 --> 00:12:03.470
you to configure your scaling
limits and your spend limits,

00:12:03.470 --> 00:12:05.120
and BigQuery takes
care of the rest.

00:12:05.120 --> 00:12:08.570
We will certainly scale up and
down your workloads, depending

00:12:08.570 --> 00:12:10.185
on what you throw at BigQuery.

00:12:10.185 --> 00:12:12.560
So some of the things that
are really relevant about this

00:12:12.560 --> 00:12:15.260
is the fact that this supports
the auto pause and the auto

00:12:15.260 --> 00:12:16.190
start mode.

00:12:16.190 --> 00:12:18.890
So we can go from
zero to full scale

00:12:18.890 --> 00:12:21.130
and back to zero
if you like that.

00:12:21.130 --> 00:12:23.180
Because we are not
powered by VMs,

00:12:23.180 --> 00:12:25.020
you get a lot of
additional advancements.

00:12:25.020 --> 00:12:28.670
So for example, we don't
have this pre-warm period

00:12:28.670 --> 00:12:30.660
when you're not getting
optimal performance.

00:12:30.660 --> 00:12:32.270
The second you
get your capacity,

00:12:32.270 --> 00:12:34.700
performance is at 100%.

00:12:34.700 --> 00:12:37.490
Also, if you have queries
in flight, queries

00:12:37.490 --> 00:12:40.082
that are being executed,
you can add capacity,

00:12:40.082 --> 00:12:41.540
and those queries
would immediately

00:12:41.540 --> 00:12:43.310
start taking advantage of that.

00:12:43.310 --> 00:12:45.950
So you can accelerate
queries in flight

00:12:45.950 --> 00:12:48.520
using BigQuery slots autoscale.

00:12:48.520 --> 00:12:51.520
Because we don't have VMs,
we're not powered by--

00:12:51.520 --> 00:12:53.410
we're not powered by local disk.

00:12:53.410 --> 00:12:55.450
So we don't have those
local disk bottlenecks.

00:12:55.450 --> 00:12:57.250
If you start running
out of local disk,

00:12:57.250 --> 00:12:59.740
you start getting performance
clips that are unpredictable.

00:12:59.740 --> 00:13:01.930
They're hard to monitor
for and hard to document.

00:13:01.930 --> 00:13:03.680
We don't have such problems.

00:13:03.680 --> 00:13:05.828
We always have hot data for you.

00:13:05.828 --> 00:13:07.870
And finally, we kind of
already talked about this

00:13:07.870 --> 00:13:11.920
in the SLA slides, but we
can provide a high level

00:13:11.920 --> 00:13:15.730
of reliability because we're
immune to individual node

00:13:15.730 --> 00:13:16.420
downtime.

00:13:16.420 --> 00:13:19.570
In fact, a huge portion of
a data center can come down,

00:13:19.570 --> 00:13:23.030
and you're not even
going to notice that.

00:13:23.030 --> 00:13:25.795
The next feature we're
launching in administration

00:13:25.795 --> 00:13:28.470
in the next few months
is admin panel UI.

00:13:28.470 --> 00:13:30.470
Now, we have stack driver
from [INAUDIBLE] which

00:13:30.470 --> 00:13:32.660
is incredibly powerful,
but our customers

00:13:32.660 --> 00:13:36.550
want us to also have BigQuery
native monitoring capability.

00:13:36.550 --> 00:13:39.110
So Admin Panel will provide
you with the ability

00:13:39.110 --> 00:13:42.330
to monitor your queries,
your slots, your resources,

00:13:42.330 --> 00:13:45.670
your spent, your workloads, and
your users right in BigQuery

00:13:45.670 --> 00:13:47.180
from BigQuery.

00:13:47.180 --> 00:13:49.110
This will have a real
time component to it,

00:13:49.110 --> 00:13:51.380
so you can see what's
happening right this second.

00:13:51.380 --> 00:13:54.720
And you can go back in time
several weeks and months.

00:13:54.720 --> 00:13:56.698
So how are you going
to use Admin Panel?

00:13:56.698 --> 00:13:58.490
Well, you're going to
be able to figure out

00:13:58.490 --> 00:14:01.520
how your organization is
using BigQuery in general.

00:14:01.520 --> 00:14:02.993
Who are your top users?

00:14:02.993 --> 00:14:04.910
What are the types of
queries they're running?

00:14:04.910 --> 00:14:07.670
And you're also going to be able
to perform real time root cause

00:14:07.670 --> 00:14:10.340
analysis whenever
things go wrong.

00:14:10.340 --> 00:14:14.250
Maybe there is a for
loop that went awry.

00:14:14.250 --> 00:14:17.240
So you want to catch that, and
you figure out what's going on,

00:14:17.240 --> 00:14:19.920
and move on.

00:14:19.920 --> 00:14:23.890
Customers always ask us,
you have all these pricing

00:14:23.890 --> 00:14:28.020
capabilities, but how do I
choose which model to use?

00:14:28.020 --> 00:14:30.660
Which one is the
right one for me?

00:14:30.660 --> 00:14:34.330
And how much capacity
should I purchase?

00:14:34.330 --> 00:14:36.450
So we're launching
BigQuery slot Recommender.

00:14:36.450 --> 00:14:38.520
With BigQuery slot
Recommender we're

00:14:38.520 --> 00:14:40.500
going to run this
intelligence, and we're

00:14:40.500 --> 00:14:43.230
going to send you
insights automatically

00:14:43.230 --> 00:14:45.540
that will actually
recommend whether you should

00:14:45.540 --> 00:14:47.850
be on the on demand
model, or whether you

00:14:47.850 --> 00:14:49.230
should be on the slots model.

00:14:49.230 --> 00:14:51.390
And of course, we're
going to do this

00:14:51.390 --> 00:14:52.975
without sacrificing performance.

00:14:52.975 --> 00:14:54.600
So we're going to
tell you that there's

00:14:54.600 --> 00:14:56.040
a recommendation out there.

00:14:56.040 --> 00:14:59.700
It's going to recommend you
to save money without actually

00:14:59.700 --> 00:15:01.770
sacrificing performance.

00:15:01.770 --> 00:15:03.360
So we talked about availability.

00:15:03.360 --> 00:15:05.340
We talked about administration.

00:15:05.340 --> 00:15:08.220
We talked about performance,
but probably the most important

00:15:08.220 --> 00:15:12.510
aspect of BigQuery
is its security.

00:15:12.510 --> 00:15:15.850
Our customers trust us with
their data and their workloads,

00:15:15.850 --> 00:15:17.880
and we can't fail them.

00:15:17.880 --> 00:15:21.850
So we have a kind of a
three-pronged strategy here.

00:15:21.850 --> 00:15:25.260
The first one is BigQuery
is a part of Google,

00:15:25.260 --> 00:15:29.310
and Google employs thousands
of the best security

00:15:29.310 --> 00:15:30.990
engineers in the world.

00:15:30.990 --> 00:15:36.480
They're working on security,
on custom hardware, networking,

00:15:36.480 --> 00:15:38.840
penetration, so on and so forth.

00:15:38.840 --> 00:15:42.420
And BigQuery gets to benefit
from all of this innovation.

00:15:42.420 --> 00:15:45.050
That's the beauty of BigQuery.

00:15:45.050 --> 00:15:46.050
That's the first pillar.

00:15:46.050 --> 00:15:48.480
So you just get a
high level of security

00:15:48.480 --> 00:15:49.930
automatically with BigQuery.

00:15:49.930 --> 00:15:51.750
The second pillar is
the fact that we're

00:15:51.750 --> 00:15:54.900
giving you a number of
controls, and configurations,

00:15:54.900 --> 00:15:59.100
and services that enable you
to customize how much security

00:15:59.100 --> 00:16:01.640
you want in your environment--
things like Sudhir

00:16:01.640 --> 00:16:03.630
mentioned customer
managed encryption keys,

00:16:03.630 --> 00:16:05.640
virtual private networking.

00:16:05.640 --> 00:16:09.310
But the key to this
component is Data Catalog.

00:16:09.310 --> 00:16:12.900
Data Catalog is a metadata
management and discovery

00:16:12.900 --> 00:16:14.760
service that we
launched recently.

00:16:14.760 --> 00:16:17.390
So imagine your data
coming into Google Cloud.

00:16:17.390 --> 00:16:20.680
Data Catalog can
automatically index that data.

00:16:20.680 --> 00:16:22.830
Then the data passes
through data loss prevention

00:16:22.830 --> 00:16:25.410
API that removes
and tokenizes PII

00:16:25.410 --> 00:16:28.110
information and private
and secure information.

00:16:28.110 --> 00:16:32.250
And then you can apply tags
and policies to this data,

00:16:32.250 --> 00:16:35.490
and grant access to the
right levels of data

00:16:35.490 --> 00:16:38.950
to the right users inside
of your organization.

00:16:38.950 --> 00:16:42.480
So it's a super flexible,
super powerful platform

00:16:42.480 --> 00:16:46.560
for data management that
allows you to really control

00:16:46.560 --> 00:16:50.420
how your data is exposed
internally and externally.

00:16:50.420 --> 00:16:52.950
And the third pillar that's
critical to our success

00:16:52.950 --> 00:16:54.410
here is the fact
that we're working

00:16:54.410 --> 00:16:58.170
with all the relevant agencies
to achieve these audits

00:16:58.170 --> 00:16:59.570
and certifications.

00:16:59.570 --> 00:17:02.160
So if you're storing medical
data inside of BigQuery,

00:17:02.160 --> 00:17:03.950
you know you can
trust us because we

00:17:03.950 --> 00:17:05.510
have HIPAA certification.

00:17:05.510 --> 00:17:07.819
If you're storing financial
data inside of BigQuery,

00:17:07.819 --> 00:17:09.470
you know you can
trust us because we

00:17:09.470 --> 00:17:11.119
have TCI certification.

00:17:11.119 --> 00:17:13.430
If you're doing business
in Germany or Singapore,

00:17:13.430 --> 00:17:15.680
we have proper
credentials, as well.

00:17:15.680 --> 00:17:20.250
And with FedRAMP we can do
business with government.

00:17:20.250 --> 00:17:22.089
SUDHIR HASBE: With
that, now we talked

00:17:22.089 --> 00:17:23.589
about proven dependability.

00:17:23.589 --> 00:17:26.560
Let's switch gears, talk
about the intelligence.

00:17:26.560 --> 00:17:28.840
One of our key capabilities
in Google Cloud

00:17:28.840 --> 00:17:32.800
is, of course, our capabilities
around machine learning and AI.

00:17:32.800 --> 00:17:34.780
And we will talk
more about how we

00:17:34.780 --> 00:17:38.170
are enhancing that embedded
machine learning capabilities

00:17:38.170 --> 00:17:39.890
that we are providing
with BigQuery,

00:17:39.890 --> 00:17:42.340
but also talk about some real
time analytics improvements

00:17:42.340 --> 00:17:43.550
that we have done.

00:17:43.550 --> 00:17:46.820
But before that, let's talk
about democratizing insights.

00:17:46.820 --> 00:17:49.750
One of our key value
pillars in Google Cloud

00:17:49.750 --> 00:17:52.460
is we want insights
for everyone.

00:17:52.460 --> 00:17:55.000
We want to make sure
every individual person

00:17:55.000 --> 00:17:57.790
in an organization can
get access to the insights

00:17:57.790 --> 00:17:58.850
that they want.

00:17:58.850 --> 00:18:01.060
So in this realm, the first
thing that we are doing

00:18:01.060 --> 00:18:03.905
is our refresh to BigQuery UI.

00:18:03.905 --> 00:18:05.530
A lot of our customers
have been asking

00:18:05.530 --> 00:18:09.540
for capabilities in BigQuery
UI, like multi type editing.

00:18:09.540 --> 00:18:10.900
We are adding that.

00:18:10.900 --> 00:18:13.660
So it's now available
for everyone to use.

00:18:13.660 --> 00:18:15.490
It's a really great
experience where

00:18:15.490 --> 00:18:18.440
you can write multiple
different queries, compare them.

00:18:18.440 --> 00:18:23.390
But more importantly, you not
only write SQL for BigQuery.

00:18:23.390 --> 00:18:27.430
You can also write data flow SQL
and create streaming pipelines

00:18:27.430 --> 00:18:31.640
with Pub/Sub and Dataflow in
combination with BigQuery.

00:18:31.640 --> 00:18:35.020
So it does allow you that
that integrated experience.

00:18:35.020 --> 00:18:37.720
It's a faster ID, and one
of the other features I like

00:18:37.720 --> 00:18:39.610
is this auto complete for me.

00:18:39.610 --> 00:18:42.100
So it can recommend
or suggest what

00:18:42.100 --> 00:18:44.740
are the table or fields
that I should be using,

00:18:44.740 --> 00:18:45.580
and stuff like that.

00:18:45.580 --> 00:18:50.190
So a lot of innovation and
investment going in that space.

00:18:50.190 --> 00:18:51.130
And the second thing--

00:18:51.130 --> 00:18:53.090
I'm super excited
about this feature,

00:18:53.090 --> 00:18:54.850
which is Connected Sheets.

00:18:54.850 --> 00:18:58.420
Connected Sheets is literally
think about Google Sheet

00:18:58.420 --> 00:19:00.880
in front of BigQuery.

00:19:00.880 --> 00:19:03.970
You can have petabyte scale
data sets in BigQuery.

00:19:03.970 --> 00:19:05.740
You can use Sheets to
go ahead and create

00:19:05.740 --> 00:19:10.570
pivot tables, charts, our
functions, and our custom

00:19:10.570 --> 00:19:12.460
calculations on
top of the sheet.

00:19:12.460 --> 00:19:15.070
And then all of that actually
runs on top of BigQuery.

00:19:15.070 --> 00:19:18.910
So it's best of both
worlds-- the great experience

00:19:18.910 --> 00:19:21.580
with great analytical engine.

00:19:21.580 --> 00:19:22.990
I personally use this every day.

00:19:22.990 --> 00:19:25.600
I have 29 plus
billion row table.

00:19:25.600 --> 00:19:29.620
I analyze data from that every
day about our product usage--

00:19:29.620 --> 00:19:32.020
what's working, what's
not working, and on.

00:19:32.020 --> 00:19:36.880
So really this
makes data at scale

00:19:36.880 --> 00:19:40.450
accessible to all analysts
who can use spreadsheets

00:19:40.450 --> 00:19:41.600
within your organization.

00:19:41.600 --> 00:19:43.870
So it's really powerful tooling.

00:19:43.870 --> 00:19:45.370
But with that, we
don't want to stop

00:19:45.370 --> 00:19:49.590
at analysts and just a few
folks in the organization.

00:19:49.590 --> 00:19:52.180
We want to extend
insights to everyone

00:19:52.180 --> 00:19:53.450
else in the organization.

00:19:53.450 --> 00:19:56.530
With that, we are announcing
availability of Data QnA.

00:19:56.530 --> 00:20:00.310
Our Data QnA is natural
language interface for BigQuery.

00:20:00.310 --> 00:20:03.890
You literally go ahead and
ask a question, get an answer.

00:20:03.890 --> 00:20:05.170
It's as simple as that.

00:20:05.170 --> 00:20:08.340
There is no set up
required on top of BigQuery

00:20:08.340 --> 00:20:09.640
for leveraging it.

00:20:09.640 --> 00:20:12.660
You just enable an API, pick the
people that you want to go to

00:20:12.660 --> 00:20:14.270
and ask questions on.

00:20:14.270 --> 00:20:16.060
And we just make
it possible for you

00:20:16.060 --> 00:20:19.210
so you can just
start leveraging it.

00:20:19.210 --> 00:20:22.390
We also believe that
no one interface

00:20:22.390 --> 00:20:25.750
is the right interface
for technology like this.

00:20:25.750 --> 00:20:29.680
I think organizations want
to build custom workflows

00:20:29.680 --> 00:20:33.370
and custom experiences on
top of such a technology.

00:20:33.370 --> 00:20:37.550
So we do have the whole
API available for Data QnA,

00:20:37.550 --> 00:20:39.760
but there are--
organizations can literally

00:20:39.760 --> 00:20:42.010
build their own experiences.

00:20:42.010 --> 00:20:44.350
But from Google's side,
we will be integrating it

00:20:44.350 --> 00:20:45.490
into Google Sheets.

00:20:45.490 --> 00:20:48.070
With Connected Sheets
plus Direct QnA,

00:20:48.070 --> 00:20:50.600
now we have a very
powerful experience.

00:20:50.600 --> 00:20:53.060
We will be providing
chat board experience.

00:20:53.060 --> 00:20:55.300
We will be working
with dialog flow

00:20:55.300 --> 00:20:59.380
and various other experiences
that we can provide directly,

00:20:59.380 --> 00:21:03.070
including BigQuery UI will
have a version of this.

00:21:03.070 --> 00:21:05.980
All of this is actually
built on an internal Google

00:21:05.980 --> 00:21:07.720
technology called Analyzer.

00:21:07.720 --> 00:21:11.630
There's a great paper that
you can read more about it on.

00:21:11.630 --> 00:21:13.780
But fundamentally, this
is the same technology

00:21:13.780 --> 00:21:18.130
that powers Google Assistant
and various other services

00:21:18.130 --> 00:21:20.890
and products that
we internally use.

00:21:20.890 --> 00:21:22.840
Analyzer gives us
the ability to go out

00:21:22.840 --> 00:21:26.050
and to question parsing,
intent identification, entity

00:21:26.050 --> 00:21:26.810
matching.

00:21:26.810 --> 00:21:29.890
All of that comes because
of Google's investment

00:21:29.890 --> 00:21:32.410
in the space of
natural language,

00:21:32.410 --> 00:21:34.890
conversational
understanding, and all.

00:21:34.890 --> 00:21:37.330
And we're just
leveraging parts of that.

00:21:37.330 --> 00:21:39.730
We are, on BigQuery's
side, making sure

00:21:39.730 --> 00:21:43.060
that that works really well
with your custom data sets,

00:21:43.060 --> 00:21:45.540
and we can make it easy
to go ahead and get

00:21:45.540 --> 00:21:48.490
started without doing
that much of preparation

00:21:48.490 --> 00:21:51.680
and set up on your data sets.

00:21:51.680 --> 00:21:55.990
Let me switch gears, talk about
embedded machine learning.

00:21:55.990 --> 00:21:59.440
One of the key values that
we have in BigQuery team

00:21:59.440 --> 00:22:04.070
is we want to bring computation
to data, not the other way

00:22:04.070 --> 00:22:04.570
around.

00:22:04.570 --> 00:22:06.700
We don't like moving
data, copying data.

00:22:06.700 --> 00:22:11.290
That's not the best way of
running large scale analytics.

00:22:11.290 --> 00:22:14.417
And so in the same realm,
80% of prime data scientists

00:22:14.417 --> 00:22:16.000
today are spending
in data preparation

00:22:16.000 --> 00:22:17.530
according to some studies.

00:22:17.530 --> 00:22:18.850
We want to avoid that.

00:22:18.850 --> 00:22:21.010
With BigQuery,
what you can do is

00:22:21.010 --> 00:22:23.050
you can just use
two lines of SQL

00:22:23.050 --> 00:22:25.440
to create machine
learning models.

00:22:25.440 --> 00:22:29.230
You literally say create
model, model name, of type,

00:22:29.230 --> 00:22:31.540
x regression, matrix
factorization,

00:22:31.540 --> 00:22:32.295
whatever you want.

00:22:32.295 --> 00:22:33.670
And then give the
input data set,

00:22:33.670 --> 00:22:36.130
and we will do everything
for you on that.

00:22:36.130 --> 00:22:38.870
We do internal
hyperparameter tuning.

00:22:38.870 --> 00:22:43.160
We can even take care of the
whole system on the back end.

00:22:43.160 --> 00:22:44.850
When we launched a
couple of years back,

00:22:44.850 --> 00:22:47.450
we only had regression,
and logistical, and linear

00:22:47.450 --> 00:22:48.650
regression models.

00:22:48.650 --> 00:22:51.500
From there, we have
come far, far ahead.

00:22:51.500 --> 00:22:54.500
We now support
k-means clustering,

00:22:54.500 --> 00:22:56.390
matrix factorization.

00:22:56.390 --> 00:22:59.630
I've seen lot of organizations
use this in combination.

00:22:59.630 --> 00:23:02.420
They do customer segmentation
using k-means clustering.

00:23:02.420 --> 00:23:04.880
They do matrix factorization
to find products

00:23:04.880 --> 00:23:06.770
that we should recommend
to those segments,

00:23:06.770 --> 00:23:09.560
and they've basically done
email campaigns and ad campaigns

00:23:09.560 --> 00:23:10.060
on them.

00:23:10.060 --> 00:23:12.500
So very common pattern.

00:23:12.500 --> 00:23:14.930
Now you can use
the use this for--

00:23:14.930 --> 00:23:18.480
in addition, we've also launched
time series forecasting.

00:23:18.480 --> 00:23:21.040
This is great for inventory
optimizations and stuff

00:23:21.040 --> 00:23:21.540
like that.

00:23:21.540 --> 00:23:25.650
So we were seeing a lot
of interest in this model,

00:23:25.650 --> 00:23:27.780
but in addition to
that, we have enabled

00:23:27.780 --> 00:23:30.950
BigQuery ML to be
the SQL interface

00:23:30.950 --> 00:23:33.260
for a lot of our
capabilities in Cloud AI.

00:23:33.260 --> 00:23:36.890
So [INAUDIBLE] classifiers, XG
posts, and auto ML tables all

00:23:36.890 --> 00:23:41.180
are using Cloud AI platform
services behind the scenes,

00:23:41.180 --> 00:23:43.730
and you still get the
same consistent experience

00:23:43.730 --> 00:23:44.570
that you have.

00:23:44.570 --> 00:23:47.180
But most importantly,
a lot of our customers

00:23:47.180 --> 00:23:49.520
said to us, hey,
all these models,

00:23:49.520 --> 00:23:52.700
creating them easily and running
batch workloads is great,

00:23:52.700 --> 00:23:56.990
but I would love to use
these models in real time

00:23:56.990 --> 00:23:58.050
predictions.

00:23:58.050 --> 00:23:59.340
How do we enable that?

00:23:59.340 --> 00:24:04.240
And so we enable you to go
ahead and export these models

00:24:04.240 --> 00:24:06.320
directly into TensorFlow
model, and so you

00:24:06.320 --> 00:24:07.970
can export it as
TensorFlow model,

00:24:07.970 --> 00:24:10.998
and then host it in any of
the serving platform that

00:24:10.998 --> 00:24:13.540
supports TensorFlow, which would
be a [INAUDIBLE],, Kubeflow.

00:24:13.540 --> 00:24:15.855
There are various platforms
that are available that you

00:24:15.855 --> 00:24:18.260
can run this with.

00:24:18.260 --> 00:24:21.530
With that, we recovered
the democratizing insights,

00:24:21.530 --> 00:24:24.140
especially with Data QnA
and Connected Sheets,

00:24:24.140 --> 00:24:25.490
embedded machine learning.

00:24:25.490 --> 00:24:27.140
The third area of
innovation that we

00:24:27.140 --> 00:24:31.670
have spent significant amount of
time is in real time analytics.

00:24:31.670 --> 00:24:34.660
As I earlier mentioned,
we have customers

00:24:34.660 --> 00:24:36.830
that are supporting in
like tens of millions

00:24:36.830 --> 00:24:39.560
of events per
second in BigQuery.

00:24:39.560 --> 00:24:44.830
And just this year, we re-wrote
our whole storage engine

00:24:44.830 --> 00:24:47.930
from batch first mode
to a streaming mode.

00:24:47.930 --> 00:24:50.840
So it's a streaming
first storage system now,

00:24:50.840 --> 00:24:52.760
and we can scale to
any of your needs.

00:24:52.760 --> 00:24:56.990
Tens of millions of events per
second is without any problem.

00:24:56.990 --> 00:24:59.450
The more important is
none of your queries

00:24:59.450 --> 00:25:01.640
will get any-- have any
impact on performance

00:25:01.640 --> 00:25:03.380
because of that ingestion rate.

00:25:03.380 --> 00:25:05.840
So we can do tens of
millions of events

00:25:05.840 --> 00:25:08.540
and give you the same query
performance in the real time,

00:25:08.540 --> 00:25:10.080
and that's coming.

00:25:10.080 --> 00:25:12.230
The second thing is
materialized views.

00:25:12.230 --> 00:25:14.930
We know, when you have a lot
of these data changes happening

00:25:14.930 --> 00:25:18.410
batch or in stream, you want
to materialize your data set

00:25:18.410 --> 00:25:21.210
and views so that
you can easily power

00:25:21.210 --> 00:25:23.930
the dashboards and
reports that you may have.

00:25:23.930 --> 00:25:25.730
All of this is
automated in BigQuery.

00:25:25.730 --> 00:25:28.790
Once you create a materialized
view, as the underlying data

00:25:28.790 --> 00:25:32.730
changes, we persisted in the
materialized view for you.

00:25:32.730 --> 00:25:34.490
What that means is you
don't have to worry

00:25:34.490 --> 00:25:37.150
about scheduling these.

00:25:37.150 --> 00:25:40.100
You will not have to worry about
when was the last update made

00:25:40.100 --> 00:25:41.480
to the base table--

00:25:41.480 --> 00:25:42.230
nothing like that.

00:25:42.230 --> 00:25:44.330
We will always
keep it consistent.

00:25:44.330 --> 00:25:47.030
And most important
is query re-routing.

00:25:47.030 --> 00:25:50.150
What that means is, if you
fire a query into BigQuery,

00:25:50.150 --> 00:25:53.420
and you created a
materialized view later,

00:25:53.420 --> 00:25:55.320
and you have this
historical query,

00:25:55.320 --> 00:25:57.320
we will automatically
take that query.

00:25:57.320 --> 00:25:59.180
Our query passing
engine will realize

00:25:59.180 --> 00:26:01.970
that you have a new
materialized view, which

00:26:01.970 --> 00:26:04.700
can make the query run faster,
and we will automatically

00:26:04.700 --> 00:26:05.850
leverage that.

00:26:05.850 --> 00:26:07.220
So that's there.

00:26:07.220 --> 00:26:09.930
It's available in beta
form for you to use now.

00:26:09.930 --> 00:26:12.710
VI engine is another
third investment in always

00:26:12.710 --> 00:26:14.660
fast, always fresh
[INAUDIBLE] for us.

00:26:14.660 --> 00:26:18.488
A BI Engine-- we launched
the BI Engine last year

00:26:18.488 --> 00:26:20.780
working within Data Studio
so that we had an integrated

00:26:20.780 --> 00:26:21.280
experience.

00:26:21.280 --> 00:26:23.180
We wanted to test
the technology,

00:26:23.180 --> 00:26:26.260
make sure it can scale to
global needs, and all of that.

00:26:26.260 --> 00:26:27.410
So I think that is there.

00:26:27.410 --> 00:26:32.500
It provides you a sub-second
latency query capability.

00:26:32.500 --> 00:26:34.040
It automatically
tunes your queries

00:26:34.040 --> 00:26:35.460
as they come in and all.

00:26:35.460 --> 00:26:37.580
But more importantly,
in this year,

00:26:37.580 --> 00:26:40.700
we are going to extend that
capability of BI Engine, which

00:26:40.700 --> 00:26:44.420
is out in memory caching engine,
to all the queries in BigQuery.

00:26:44.420 --> 00:26:46.640
So when the query comes
in, it will be able

00:26:46.640 --> 00:26:50.580
to leverage BigQuery, even from
a standard BigQuery interface.

00:26:50.580 --> 00:26:53.300
In addition to that, all
your JDBC ODBC drivers

00:26:53.300 --> 00:26:54.330
will support it.

00:26:54.330 --> 00:26:56.600
And also, all of
our BI partners,

00:26:56.600 --> 00:27:00.950
like Tableau, Looker,
Sisense, Domo, Power BI--

00:27:00.950 --> 00:27:04.430
all of them will be able
to leverage the BI Engine

00:27:04.430 --> 00:27:05.720
capability that we have.

00:27:05.720 --> 00:27:08.090
We just pick what
your size of cache is,

00:27:08.090 --> 00:27:09.960
and everything will
automatically work.

00:27:09.960 --> 00:27:13.270
And then also one of the
other things we will support

00:27:13.270 --> 00:27:15.460
is star schemas so
that all your BI work

00:27:15.460 --> 00:27:17.720
will start-- taken care of.

00:27:17.720 --> 00:27:21.330
Extending that, if you really
think from a stack perspective,

00:27:21.330 --> 00:27:24.390
you, as customers today,
now have a choice.

00:27:24.390 --> 00:27:27.740
You can decide whether
you want low latency,

00:27:27.740 --> 00:27:31.500
or you're OK with
relatively higher latency.

00:27:31.500 --> 00:27:33.080
And you can decide
how much you want

00:27:33.080 --> 00:27:35.910
to use on the very hard
data with caching the--

00:27:35.910 --> 00:27:38.500
with BI Engine, or you just
want to create materialized

00:27:38.500 --> 00:27:40.993
view, which we will
keep fresh all the time.

00:27:40.993 --> 00:27:42.660
Or you want to just
use partition tables

00:27:42.660 --> 00:27:44.890
or just go to the
storage layer directly.

00:27:44.890 --> 00:27:47.460
So all of this is a
choice that you have,

00:27:47.460 --> 00:27:49.020
but you write the query.

00:27:49.020 --> 00:27:51.660
We will automatically
first try BI Engine.

00:27:51.660 --> 00:27:52.390
Not there.

00:27:52.390 --> 00:27:53.370
Go to materialize view.

00:27:53.370 --> 00:27:53.870
Not there.

00:27:53.870 --> 00:27:55.630
Go to partitioning
and clustering.

00:27:55.630 --> 00:27:59.300
If not, go to the cold storage,
however the data is stored.

00:27:59.300 --> 00:28:01.770
So that's the key thing that
we have been working on.

00:28:01.770 --> 00:28:05.160
You will always get the best
performance at the best price.

00:28:05.160 --> 00:28:09.260
So you can pick and choose
how-- what you care about.

00:28:09.260 --> 00:28:10.720
With that, we
covered intelligence

00:28:10.720 --> 00:28:12.760
and proven dependability.

00:28:12.760 --> 00:28:15.870
Let's switch gears and talk
about open and flexible.

00:28:15.870 --> 00:28:17.810
This is another
big theme for us.

00:28:17.810 --> 00:28:20.980
We'll cover BigQuery
everywhere, flexible pricing,

00:28:20.980 --> 00:28:24.070
as well as data platform
interoperability in this.

00:28:24.070 --> 00:28:26.460
Let's talk about BigQuery--

00:28:26.460 --> 00:28:27.700
BigQuery everywhere.

00:28:27.700 --> 00:28:29.590
We are introducing
BigQuery Omni.

00:28:29.590 --> 00:28:30.880
What is BigQuery Omni?

00:28:30.880 --> 00:28:34.240
As I earlier said,
we have this theory--

00:28:34.240 --> 00:28:40.170
vision in-- in BigQuery where we
like to bring compute to data.

00:28:40.170 --> 00:28:43.240
A lot of our customers
have data not in GCP.

00:28:43.240 --> 00:28:46.480
A lot of them have
data in AWS S3.

00:28:46.480 --> 00:28:48.760
And maybe other
customers have in Azure.

00:28:48.760 --> 00:28:52.420
So with BigQuery Omni
available for AWS,

00:28:52.420 --> 00:28:55.600
we will allow you to write
queries, and execute,

00:28:55.600 --> 00:28:59.470
and do analysis on top of
the data that's stored in S3.

00:28:59.470 --> 00:29:00.400
What does this mean?

00:29:00.400 --> 00:29:03.090
We will literally run
our compute engine--

00:29:03.090 --> 00:29:06.290
our computation engine
or BigQuery in AWS

00:29:06.290 --> 00:29:07.708
close up through your data.

00:29:07.708 --> 00:29:09.250
And what that means
is you don't have

00:29:09.250 --> 00:29:12.520
to worry about paying for this
egress charges or anything.

00:29:12.520 --> 00:29:14.980
So you may have
tens of to hundreds

00:29:14.980 --> 00:29:16.610
of terabytes of data in S3.

00:29:16.610 --> 00:29:19.010
You can stop writing queries
with the same interface

00:29:19.010 --> 00:29:22.840
in BigQuery or in the same
SQL dialect that we support,

00:29:22.840 --> 00:29:25.580
as well as--

00:29:25.580 --> 00:29:26.920
and run it on AWS.

00:29:26.920 --> 00:29:30.780
So you'll be able to
do computation in that,

00:29:30.780 --> 00:29:34.320
and then get access
to the results.

00:29:34.320 --> 00:29:35.950
There are two or
three different kinds

00:29:35.950 --> 00:29:39.460
of use cases that are becoming
really prevalent in this.

00:29:39.460 --> 00:29:42.300
And a lot of our customers
do have split mode.

00:29:42.300 --> 00:29:45.730
A lot our customers use BigQuery
and GCP, as well as they

00:29:45.730 --> 00:29:47.800
have data in S3 and AWS.

00:29:47.800 --> 00:29:50.500
This is going to allow you
to have a single interface

00:29:50.500 --> 00:29:52.360
to analyze data across clouds.

00:29:52.360 --> 00:29:54.610
And as I said, we
will be bringing out

00:29:54.610 --> 00:29:58.380
the BigQuery Omni for
Azure in coming quarters,

00:29:58.380 --> 00:30:01.060
so that will extend it
to that platform, too.

00:30:01.060 --> 00:30:02.320
So that's one.

00:30:02.320 --> 00:30:04.930
The second use case that is
becoming pretty prevalent

00:30:04.930 --> 00:30:08.620
is I've been talking to
large customers who have tens

00:30:08.620 --> 00:30:11.200
to hundreds of petabytes in S3.

00:30:11.200 --> 00:30:13.810
They don't use GCP 3,
but they are intrigued

00:30:13.810 --> 00:30:18.310
with our capabilities like the
BigQuery ML embedded machine

00:30:18.310 --> 00:30:20.450
learning capability,
and they want to use it.

00:30:20.450 --> 00:30:22.060
And so in this
case, what we can do

00:30:22.060 --> 00:30:24.880
is we can enable those
organizations to do data

00:30:24.880 --> 00:30:27.430
preparation,
summarizing the data,

00:30:27.430 --> 00:30:29.590
identifying the key data
sets that are there,

00:30:29.590 --> 00:30:33.190
and then just move the data
that you want to run machine

00:30:33.190 --> 00:30:36.850
learning on to GCP in BigQuery,
and then let those machine

00:30:36.850 --> 00:30:40.225
learning models do
advanced analytics with it.

00:30:40.225 --> 00:30:42.100
So those are the different
kinds of use cases

00:30:42.100 --> 00:30:46.100
we are seeing in the early
adoption that we have seen.

00:30:46.100 --> 00:30:48.580
We are learning as we go,
and we would love for you

00:30:48.580 --> 00:30:52.240
to request access, get
access to this technology.

00:30:52.240 --> 00:30:56.040
And would love to co-innovate
with you in the space.

00:30:56.040 --> 00:31:00.100
With that, let's
talk about pricing.

00:31:00.100 --> 00:31:02.310
TINO TERESHKO: So we
talked about how BigQuery

00:31:02.310 --> 00:31:05.190
has some of the largest
use cases in the world,

00:31:05.190 --> 00:31:07.470
but what's interesting
is BigQuery probably

00:31:07.470 --> 00:31:10.750
also has some of the smallest
use cases in the world.

00:31:10.750 --> 00:31:13.740
And it's really because
we build our system

00:31:13.740 --> 00:31:17.460
to be scale invariate, and we
build the right pricing options

00:31:17.460 --> 00:31:21.370
to support that kind of
gradient of scenarios.

00:31:21.370 --> 00:31:24.330
So no matter where you are in
terms of scale, capability,

00:31:24.330 --> 00:31:27.720
and requirements, we're going
to have the right pricing

00:31:27.720 --> 00:31:28.890
options for you.

00:31:28.890 --> 00:31:30.660
So we do have a perpetual
free tier, where

00:31:30.660 --> 00:31:32.970
you get 10 gigabytes
of storage for free,

00:31:32.970 --> 00:31:35.130
and a whole terabyte
of execution-- query

00:31:35.130 --> 00:31:38.633
execution, query processing
for free, no strings attached.

00:31:38.633 --> 00:31:40.050
And we have pay
as you go pricing,

00:31:40.050 --> 00:31:42.690
which is serverless pricing,
incredibly efficient.

00:31:42.690 --> 00:31:44.780
You don't have to
think about anything.

00:31:44.780 --> 00:31:48.090
But as you kind of graduate,
and become bigger and bigger,

00:31:48.090 --> 00:31:51.090
you start caring about
additional capacity.

00:31:51.090 --> 00:31:53.307
You start caring about
price predictability.

00:31:53.307 --> 00:31:55.140
So we have flex slots
there that we recently

00:31:55.140 --> 00:31:57.780
launched, and then all the way
out through the largest use

00:31:57.780 --> 00:31:58.680
cases.

00:31:58.680 --> 00:32:00.180
If you really care
about efficiency,

00:32:00.180 --> 00:32:03.150
you care about price
predictability,

00:32:03.150 --> 00:32:06.120
and you're working on these
types of annual budgets,

00:32:06.120 --> 00:32:07.923
we have flat rate
pricing for you.

00:32:07.923 --> 00:32:10.590
So wherever you are, BigQuery is
going to have the right pricing

00:32:10.590 --> 00:32:12.540
options for you.

00:32:12.540 --> 00:32:14.990
And of course, you're
not just using BigQuery.

00:32:14.990 --> 00:32:17.630
Typically our customers are
running Sparkwork, [INAUDIBLE],,

00:32:17.630 --> 00:32:21.810
data pandas, data frames, other
types of execution engines.

00:32:21.810 --> 00:32:23.780
And at the same time,
they keep their data

00:32:23.780 --> 00:32:25.910
in other places other
than BigQuery storage.

00:32:25.910 --> 00:32:28.340
So whether it's MySQL,
Postgres, CloudSQL--

00:32:28.340 --> 00:32:30.410
it could be a
potentially Bigtable.

00:32:30.410 --> 00:32:34.250
It could be Google Drive,
and it could be open data

00:32:34.250 --> 00:32:36.740
lakes like Parquet and ORC.

00:32:36.740 --> 00:32:40.570
So we provide interoperability
with all of these layers.

00:32:40.570 --> 00:32:44.000
So BigQuery storage API and
its ecosystem of open source

00:32:44.000 --> 00:32:47.960
connectors allow you to run
Spark, pandas, MapReduce,

00:32:47.960 --> 00:32:50.660
and other open source
workloads directly

00:32:50.660 --> 00:32:54.560
on top of BigQuery storage
at high levels of throughput

00:32:54.560 --> 00:32:55.760
and parallelism.

00:32:55.760 --> 00:32:59.180
So it's as if you're running
these workloads directly

00:32:59.180 --> 00:33:02.600
on open storage,
directly on HDFS

00:33:02.600 --> 00:33:04.130
without sacrificing performance.

00:33:04.130 --> 00:33:06.440
It's a very unique and
differentiated capability

00:33:06.440 --> 00:33:07.970
of BigQuery.

00:33:07.970 --> 00:33:12.170
Now, if you want to
leverage BigQuery SQL,

00:33:12.170 --> 00:33:14.540
but your data lives
outside of BigQuery,

00:33:14.540 --> 00:33:16.160
we provide that capability.

00:33:16.160 --> 00:33:19.910
So CloudSQL Federation
allows you to, in real time,

00:33:19.910 --> 00:33:22.250
access data in your
relational databases

00:33:22.250 --> 00:33:25.350
from BigQuery execution engine
using your familiar BigQuery

00:33:25.350 --> 00:33:26.070
SQL.

00:33:26.070 --> 00:33:28.740
And, of course, we support
Parquet and ORC Federation,

00:33:28.740 --> 00:33:30.950
which means that you can
run BigQuery right on top

00:33:30.950 --> 00:33:32.000
of your data lakes.

00:33:35.440 --> 00:33:38.470
And finally, your data may be
living in these other service

00:33:38.470 --> 00:33:41.050
providers, like Stripe, Marketo.

00:33:41.050 --> 00:33:44.080
Maybe you have
Google internal data

00:33:44.080 --> 00:33:46.810
sets to live within
AdWords, within YouTube,

00:33:46.810 --> 00:33:47.988
and so on and so forth.

00:33:47.988 --> 00:33:50.030
How do you get that data
into your data warehouse

00:33:50.030 --> 00:33:51.850
so you can enrich it
and you can join it

00:33:51.850 --> 00:33:53.850
against your other
relevant business data?

00:33:53.850 --> 00:33:55.870
Well, we provide it--
we provide hundreds

00:33:55.870 --> 00:33:58.140
of connectors that are
very easy to configure,

00:33:58.140 --> 00:33:59.720
very easy to set up.

00:33:59.720 --> 00:34:01.150
And we take care of the rest.

00:34:01.150 --> 00:34:03.940
Once your connection
is set up, we simply

00:34:03.940 --> 00:34:06.250
move that data into
BigQuery on your behalf,

00:34:06.250 --> 00:34:08.633
and now you can do
advanced analytics on it.

00:34:08.633 --> 00:34:09.550
SUDHIR HASBE: Perfect.

